00:00:56.875 --> 00:00:59.500
Maybe you're one of those people
who just want a well-worn path

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:00.100
to follow.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.010
When I was doing
consulting, we had one way

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.957
that we shipped
most of our web apps

00:01:03.957 --> 00:01:05.540
because we knew it,
we knew it worked,

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.030
we knew how to do it right.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:08.770
And so we used it
as much as we could.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:09.970
It was our well-worn path.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:11.720
So I'm going to try
to give you some ideas

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.450
of some well-worn paths on GCP.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.910
And this talk is especially
for folks with minimal DevOps

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.510
experience who want to
know just enough that they

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:22.090
can make cool things and make
them available for other people

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:23.920
to use them.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.340
So what's this talk about?

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.055
The title is decidedly vague.

00:01:27.055 --> 00:01:28.555
This talk is about
"productionizing"

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:29.770
and application.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.290
We're going to talk about
your compute options on GCP.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:33.831
We're going to talk
about monitoring,

00:01:33.831 --> 00:01:36.220
application performance
monitoring, specifically.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.512
And then, at the very end,
there's going to be a recap.

00:01:38.512 --> 00:01:40.428
We're going to give all
the information again,

00:01:40.428 --> 00:01:41.710
so that you can see it twice.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.500
And the AV team has been really
good about getting the talks

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.724
up on YouTube very, very
quickly so you don't feel

00:01:46.724 --> 00:01:47.890
like you have to take notes.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.306
You will likely be able to see
this again at your own pace

00:01:50.306 --> 00:01:52.235
tomorrow.

00:01:52.235 --> 00:01:54.610
And so I'm going to do something
a little different here.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.260
Every time I've given a
talk like this in the past,

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.900
I've been, like, this is
one of your compute options,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.870
this is what it's good at,
this is what it's not good at.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.310
But that didn't really work
for me with a narrative flow.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.310
I found it really boring,
and it didn't actually

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.330
help folks make good decisions.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:11.650
So I flipped that on
its head for this talk.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.272
And the entire talk is going
to be based around questions.

00:02:14.272 --> 00:02:15.730
I have seven
questions that I think

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.438
everyone should ask
themselves if they're

00:02:17.438 --> 00:02:18.970
taking an app to production.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:20.720
And they're not yes
or no questions,

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.870
so they're going to make
you think a little bit.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:24.940
I hope to eventually do a blog
series about these questions

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:25.900
because there's a
lot more that I'd

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.510
like to cover that I can't
get to in the time allowed.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.070
So please bear with
me if the format

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:31.150
doesn't work for your brain.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.470
Hopefully, for some of
you, it works better

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:33.511
than the standard format.

00:02:33.511 --> 00:02:36.420
It works really well for me.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.180
So question number one--

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.860
question number one is,
what are my priorities?

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.540
If you're shipping something,
you have priorities.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.110
And priorities are super
important because everything

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:50.010
in the DevOps/SRE
world is a trade-off.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.750
The most secure,
bug-free website

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.000
is one that doesn't exist,
and doesn't have any users,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.380
and was never published.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.520
And none of us want that.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.290
So you're going to have to
have some downtime probably--

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.810
maybe not a lot, maybe only
milliseconds every year.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:06.090
But you're going to
probably have some downtime.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.360
You're going to have to make
some sacrifices of how fast you

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.670
can move for robustness
or maybe how much you're

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:15.330
willing to spend for how easy
to use your application platform

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:16.380
is.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:19.300
So you need to think
about your priorities.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.390
Some examples of priorities is,
maybe you're very early stage,

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.481
and you're demoing to
potential investors.

00:03:24.481 --> 00:03:24.980
OK.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:26.200
There's a different
set of priorities

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.810
for that than if you're in
beta testing or early access

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.360
or maybe even ready to go live.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.940
Maybe your biggest priority
is saving money-- no shame.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.090
No shame in saving money.

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:35.970
I have worked at a startup.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.220
I've worked at a startup
that nearly ran out of money.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:38.759
I get it.

00:03:38.759 --> 00:03:41.050
But you have to make sure
that you're aware of the fact

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.216
that that's your priority,
so you can make decisions

00:03:43.216 --> 00:03:44.429
with that in mind.

00:03:44.429 --> 00:03:45.970
Or maybe your priority
is robustness.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:47.386
Maybe you are
working on something

00:03:47.386 --> 00:03:50.084
where any downtime is
going to be a problem.

00:03:50.084 --> 00:03:52.000
Well, you're going to
make some decisions that

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.830
are based on that,
and they're going

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:55.510
to have other
consequences elsewhere.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.850
So think really hard about
what your priorities are.

00:03:57.850 --> 00:03:59.380
Make sure you put
them in some sort

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.534
of approximate,
stack-ranked order

00:04:01.534 --> 00:04:02.950
because those
priorities are going

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:04.970
to influence your choices.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:07.420
Your compute environment--
well, some compute environments

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:08.380
are cheaper.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.840
Generally, not always
true, but frequently,

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.240
more-managed solutions are
slightly more expensive

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:16.839
than less-managed solutions,
but you're sacrificing time.

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:19.072
Maybe that's the right
trade-off for you.

00:04:19.072 --> 00:04:21.529
If you need a lot of
robustness, maybe you

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:23.390
need a lot of monitoring.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:27.050
If you mostly don't
care, maybe you don't.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.414
If you're early stage,
perhaps that's all you need.

00:04:29.414 --> 00:04:30.830
Maybe you work in
an industry that

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:32.600
has stringent
audit-logging requirements.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:36.030
Well, logging is going
to be a priority for you.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.540
Maybe you don't write
particularly good code like me,

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.847
or you know you're jamming
this out at the last minute,

00:04:41.847 --> 00:04:43.930
and so there's going to
be problems in production.

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:46.950
So being able to debug
or trace and figure out

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.320
what you did that's
making everything explode,

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:53.010
like my demo downstairs did
yesterday, is important.

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:54.561
And maybe you need
disaster recovery.

00:04:54.561 --> 00:04:57.060
This is something that nobody
thinks of until something goes

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:58.677
wrong, like, there's an outage.

00:04:58.677 --> 00:05:00.760
And then you're like, oh
right, that's the thing--

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.229
yeah, I was supposed to do that.

00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:04.020
So make sure you think
about that up front.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.510
A lot of places don't need
it, especially early on.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.800
But you might get to
a point where you do.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:14.090
So that was question one--
priorities-- nothing actually

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:15.320
technical there.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:18.480
We're going to get slightly
more technical as we go on.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.660
The second question is,
what does my load look like?

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.850
So I've worked at a bunch
of different places.

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.420
I've probably had every job
title that's common in tech

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:27.800
at this point.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:30.162
And I had a company I worked
at where we only got load

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:32.620
during the school day because
we were an education product.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.880
So weekends-- no use.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.560
Midnight-- no use.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.170
9:00 to 3:00-- lots of use.

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:40.871
So we had different
load requirements

00:05:40.871 --> 00:05:43.370
than an app that has a pretty
steady use case or an app that

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.680
might have special work
it does late at night

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.100
to do a bunch of computations or
a bunch of predictive modeling.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:52.070
I worked at this website once
where we were doing a release,

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.410
and we had done a
green-blue-type deployment.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:55.820
We didn't call it
that back then,

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:58.040
because I've been
doing this for a while.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:01.100
And we realized that
9:00 AM on Saturdays

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.200
was our highest load we
ever got during the week.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.810
And that's when we were trying
to bring the site back up.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:07.940
And half of the cluster
was not actually

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.790
sufficient to support the
entire load, so we went down.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:14.100
So that was a good lesson for
us to learn about load, also

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.701
a good lesson for us to
learn about headspace.

00:06:16.701 --> 00:06:18.950
But you need to think about
what your load looks like.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:21.116
And this is a question you
should be asking yourself

00:06:21.116 --> 00:06:22.290
on a somewhat regular basis.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.790
Depending on how fast you're
growing, it could be quarterly;

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:25.970
it could be yearly.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:27.800
But you need to be thinking
about what your load looks

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:30.049
like because that's going
to influence your technology

00:06:30.049 --> 00:06:31.240
choices.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:33.430
So your load is going to
influence your choices.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:34.620
I just said that.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.970
If you have a very
variable load--

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.580
so something that's a lot during
some periods and none at all

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.860
during others--
sinusoid-type thing--

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:47.050
you're going to probably want
something that's autoscaling.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:49.800
On GCP, we have lots of those.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.300
We have App Engine, which is
the one that most people know--

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:53.799
been around for a
really long time--

00:06:53.799 --> 00:06:54.820
10 years.

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:57.730
We have Google Kubernetes
Engine, which also autoscales.

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:00.100
I'm using that for one of
the installations downstairs

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.950
in the showcase floor.

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:04.960
People don't frequently
know that Google Compute

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:06.640
Engine will also autoscale.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.490
You can set up managed instance
groups on Google Compute Engine

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:11.819
and say, it should be something
between here and here.

00:07:11.819 --> 00:07:13.360
And it will auto
scale those for you.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:14.818
It will make more
as you need them,

00:07:14.818 --> 00:07:16.090
take them down as you don't.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:19.150
And my new favoritest thing
ever, Google Cloud Functions,

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:21.730
also autoscales because
it's functions as a service,

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:24.310
and that's kind of what it does.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.160
It does units of work, and
you can do lots of them

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.146
or very few of them,
and it's great.

00:07:29.146 --> 00:07:30.520
If you have periods
of time where

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:32.890
you don't want your website
up or your website probably

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:35.380
doesn't need to be up, like,
if you're in that still really

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:37.088
early-on investor
stage where you're only

00:07:37.088 --> 00:07:39.940
going to give them access for a
couple of days, scaling to zero

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:41.587
is likely important.

00:07:41.587 --> 00:07:43.420
So if you're going to
need to scale to zero,

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:47.600
your choices are App Engine
Standard or Google Cloud

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.184
Functions.

00:07:49.184 --> 00:07:51.350
Lots of people don't actually
need to scale to zero,

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:53.641
but if you need it, it's a
fantastic way to save money.

00:07:53.641 --> 00:07:57.790
So if money is a priority, you
might consider that as well.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:07:59.800
And then, if you're
setting up autoscaling,

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:00.570
you need to put bumpers.

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:02.320
You need to say, this
is the minimum size,

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.230
this is the maximum size.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:06.810
And to do that, you have to
know approximately how big

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.252
your cluster might need to get.

00:08:08.252 --> 00:08:10.710
That's why we need to know
about how many users we're going

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.499
to have on a regular basis.

00:08:12.499 --> 00:08:14.790
The factor I've always used
in my professional career--

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:17.310
and I don't know if this is
endorsed by any Google SRE,

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.260
because I am not a Google SRE--

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:22.090
is 2x of the worst
case scenario.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:24.510
Now, there is a case
study on our blog

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.140
where 5x of the
worst case scenario

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:27.990
was not actually sufficient.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:29.340
So know your use.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.270
Know how stuff's going to grow.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:35.940
I have had an app that went on
a morning TV show in the US.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.890
And we way
overprovisioned for that.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:40.260
We actually kicked it
up to 4x just in case

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:41.691
it went really, really well.

00:08:41.691 --> 00:08:43.440
And the nice thing
about autoscaling stuff

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.690
is you can say, OK, just for
this day, because we know

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:47.460
we're going to have
this news article,

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.540
let's put that top
upper limit really,

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:53.280
really high so we'll be OK
if something goes awesome,

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.200
but we don't have to pay
for the extra awesome

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.150
if things don't go so well.

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:58.609
So be aware.

00:08:58.609 --> 00:09:00.900
You need to, again, reevaluate
this on a regular basis.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:04.404
You can set those bumpers
in appropriate places.

00:09:04.404 --> 00:09:06.070
Question three--
halfway through-- there

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:06.903
are seven questions.

00:09:06.903 --> 00:09:08.620
I don't think I mentioned that.

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.850
What skills do I have?

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:13.160
So this is where you
think deep inside yourself

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:16.070
and, like, what do
I actually know?

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:18.050
Specifically, languages
and frameworks--

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:19.660
I'm a giant language dork.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:22.160
If you go to my Twitter profile
or you look at my conference

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:26.240
bio, I mentioned Prolog because
I am a giant language dork.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:28.970
But at GCP, I'm on
the Advocacy Team.

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:32.580
And one of my primary
areas of advocacy is Ruby--

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:34.551
so very different.

00:09:34.551 --> 00:09:37.050
If I was going to have to do a
database-backed website right

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:38.430
now, I would pick up Rails.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.050
I know it backwards,
forwards, and inside out.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:41.966
I know that I can get
something up and running

00:09:41.966 --> 00:09:43.410
very, very quickly.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.710
But that's not the best
solution for a lot of things.

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:48.230
Doing WebSockets in Rails,
until very recently,

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:51.242
was awful, like,
truly, truly, awful.

00:09:51.242 --> 00:09:52.700
So if I was going
to do WebSockets,

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:55.465
I would have picked up
something like NodeJS.

00:09:55.465 --> 00:09:56.840
I also have been
using Node a lot

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:59.423
because I've been falling deeply
in love with Cloud Functions,

00:09:59.423 --> 00:10:01.670
and Cloud Functions
supports Node.

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:03.470
So think about what you know.

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:06.110
If you're doing data science,
Python might be a good choice.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:08.900
Think about what you're
comfortable with.

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:11.696
Also, think about what kinds of
configurations are you used to?

00:10:11.696 --> 00:10:13.070
So I've been asking
this question

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:14.528
at all of the
conference talks I've

00:10:14.528 --> 00:10:16.880
been doing of late because
it's confusing me deeply.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:19.580
Who, here, has stood
up a VM in a cloud

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:22.550
or actually, physically,
something they racked--

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:24.200
made a VM, provisioned it?

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:26.720
OK, less than I expected,
but still there.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:29.490
Who's taken that
VM to production?

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:32.610
OK, so if I asked that
question five years ago,

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:34.850
it would have been the vast
majority of the audience.

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:36.620
And now that we're
in this wonderful age

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:38.200
of serverless and
managed services,

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:40.730
it's declining very
steadily over time.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:42.990
No shame in having not
done that, by the way.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.620
But if you've never done it,
and you don't want to learn it,

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:48.632
probably Compute Engine
is not your choice.

00:10:48.632 --> 00:10:50.090
You can also decide
to learn stuff.

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:52.100
You have to make sure
you budget extra time.

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:53.030
You have to make
sure that you have

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:55.466
resources available-- meetups,
whatever-- to learn things.

00:10:55.466 --> 00:10:57.590
And make sure you're making
that choice on purpose.

00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:59.810
I've been helping a number of
folks over the last two years

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:01.580
who are like, everyone says
I should use containers.

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:03.470
And I'm, like,
containers are awesome.

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:05.696
And they're, like, this is
my first web application.

00:11:05.696 --> 00:11:07.070
I don't know what
a Stack Traces.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:09.486
And I'm like, container is not
your good choice right now.

00:11:09.486 --> 00:11:10.600
Let's do something else.

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:11.990
Because you shouldn't
be using containers

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:13.580
unless you're comfortable
enough with your stack

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:15.290
that you can deal with the
fact that the container adds

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:16.760
an extra layer of
indirection that

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:18.320
makes everything
just slightly harder

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:19.861
if you're debugging
application code.

00:11:19.861 --> 00:11:21.584
It makes a lot of stuff easier.

00:11:21.584 --> 00:11:23.750
It's a good way to make
sure you're getting the most

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:27.560
bang for your buck out
of your infrastructure,

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:29.090
but it makes some things harder.

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:31.700
And you should make sure you go
into that with your eyes open.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:33.950
Just because everyone else
says containers are awesome

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:36.122
does not mean containers
are awesome for you.

00:11:36.122 --> 00:11:38.330
Just because everyone else
says serverless is awesome

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.932
does not mean that serverless
is awesome for you.

00:11:40.932 --> 00:11:42.890
If you went to my colleague
Brian Dorsey's talk

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:44.556
about "where do I run
my stuff" earlier,

00:11:44.556 --> 00:11:47.130
there was a giant slide
that said, "it depends."

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:48.950
And that's because it depends.

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:50.750
There is not one best way.

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:52.190
That's the thesis of this talk.

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:55.449
That's what we're going to be
talking about the entire time.

00:11:55.449 --> 00:11:56.990
So we have some
language restrictions

00:11:56.990 --> 00:11:59.087
with our compute offerings.

00:11:59.087 --> 00:12:00.920
I wish we didn't, because
my language is not

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:02.540
particularly well represented.

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:04.580
But we are adding more languages
and more support all the time,

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:05.390
and it is getting better.

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:07.723
It's so much better than it
was 3 and 1/2 years ago when

00:12:07.723 --> 00:12:09.300
I started at GCP.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.590
So Google Cloud
Functions runs Node.

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:14.240
More languages are coming soon.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:17.034
I cannot define what
soon is, I am sorry.

00:12:17.034 --> 00:12:18.950
And when they come soon,
they will be in beta.

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:20.420
And I'm here to
tell you right now,

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:23.003
you should not use beta products
in production-- please don't.

00:12:23.003 --> 00:12:24.720
It's a really bad idea.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:26.580
I don't feel like I
should have to say that,

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:29.121
but people do it all the time
because they are super excited.

00:12:29.121 --> 00:12:33.590
And then it explodes, or we
massively change the interface

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:34.180
or something.

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:37.370
So don't use beta products
in production, pretty please.

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:40.190
We have App Engine Standard,
which supports Java,

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:42.860
Python, Go, and
Node is now in beta.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:46.120
So again, don't use beta
products in production, please.

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:48.840
But hopefully, we'll be
going GA relatively soon.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:51.170
App Engine Standard
is amazing, but there

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:54.020
are some restrictions on the
libraries that you can use.

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:56.480
And both Google Cloud Functions
and App Engine Standard

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.460
don't let you write to disk.

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:00.480
It's becoming less
of an issue recently.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:01.850
But for a long time,
people were, like, well,

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:02.900
I have to write to disk.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:05.150
And I'm like, well,
you don't have to.

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:07.310
But if writing to
disk is important,

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:09.050
don't use those products.

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:10.850
If you're writing
to disk, and you're

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:12.739
doing something
that's based on HTTP,

00:13:12.739 --> 00:13:14.030
you might want to rethink that.

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:17.030
Because at the end of the day,
HTTP is, in fact, stateless.

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:19.370
And writing to disk
is preserving state.

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:22.580
So make sure that you've
reconciled those contradictions

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:24.540
in your head.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:26.570
App Engine Flex--
this is our, "Yes, we

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:28.520
can run containers,
any container you want,

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:30.910
in a managed fashion."

00:13:30.910 --> 00:13:33.410
We support all of our official
languages, all seven of them.

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.800
Let's see if I can name
the Seven Dwarves--

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:40.166
Java, Python, Go, PHP,
Node, .NET, and Ruby.

00:13:40.166 --> 00:13:41.540
But you can also
run anything you

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:42.831
can run in a container on this.

00:13:42.831 --> 00:13:44.770
Last year, at this event,
one of my colleagues

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:46.987
ran Perl in a container
as part of his talk.

00:13:46.987 --> 00:13:48.570
So really, truly,
on App Engine Flex--

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:51.680
we can run anything as long as
you can put it in a container.

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:53.210
Google Kubernetes Engine-- yes.

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:56.030
You can run anything there if
you can run it in a container.

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:58.610
And Google Compute Engine-- if
you can run it on your lappy,

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:02.204
you can run it on Compute
Engine, mostly, almost always.

00:14:02.204 --> 00:14:03.620
And there are
probably some things

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:06.120
you can do on Compute Engine
that you can't do on your lappy

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:07.820
because your lappy
fits in your bag,

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:12.520
and you can make a really big
machine on Compute Engine.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:15.400
So fourth question--
what is the happy path?

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:17.500
Who's heard the term
"happy path" before?

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:18.680
It's one of my favorites.

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:20.710
So happy path is the
thing that you want people

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:22.840
to be doing on your website.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:25.450
So if you sell stuff,
that's the path

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:26.666
where you sell them things.

00:14:26.666 --> 00:14:28.540
Because that's how you
make money, and that's

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:30.340
how your website keeps going.

00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:32.314
If you've got some sort
of social media thing,

00:14:32.314 --> 00:14:34.480
you probably want people
to log in, and view a feed,

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:36.070
and talk to their friends.

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:38.800
The happy path, if you've heard
the 80/20 rule, is the 80 case.

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:41.340
This is the stuff that
goes on most of the time.

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:44.070
Your "I forgot my password"
link is not the happy path.

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:46.060
It's important, but it
is not the happy path.

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:49.150
It's not where people are
spending most of their time.

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:50.950
And once you know what
your happy path is,

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:53.440
you need to instrument
the heck out of it.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:56.140
First thing, minimum bar--
you should have uptime checks.

00:14:56.140 --> 00:14:58.560
You should make sure that you
know if the site goes down.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:14:59.440
Because if you
don't, you're going

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:01.667
to get angry customers
or angry marketing folks.

00:15:01.667 --> 00:15:02.250
Don't do that.

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:05.410
Stackdriver Monitoring
has uptime checks

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:06.940
down at the Disruption Detector.

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:08.981
We're showing that you
can see your uptime checks

00:15:08.981 --> 00:15:11.770
in real time with latency
from all over the globe.

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:14.560
Uptime checks are awesome,
but they're boring.

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:16.060
The next thing you
should do is, you

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:18.850
should monitor the
performance of the happy path.

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:21.308
This is where people are going
to spend most of their time,

00:15:21.308 --> 00:15:23.920
so this is the stuff that needs
to be fast and consistent.

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:26.500
We have Stackdriver APM, which
is Stackdriver Application

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:28.125
Performance Monitoring,
if you've never

00:15:28.125 --> 00:15:29.954
had the APM acronym before.

00:15:29.954 --> 00:15:31.870
My favorite part of that
is Stackdriver Trace.

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:32.920
It's distributed tracing.

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:34.878
It works across microservices,
and it gives you

00:15:34.878 --> 00:15:37.690
a nice, visual, timeline-y
representation of what's

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:38.930
going on with your app.

00:15:38.930 --> 00:15:40.930
I've been doing this long
enough that I actually

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:43.810
wrote one of these out on
graph paper with crayons at one

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:45.850
of my previous jobs to
prove that we were fine

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:47.142
and didn't need more servers.

00:15:47.142 --> 00:15:48.850
But it's so much better
when the computer

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:51.350
draws the graphic for you.

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:54.460
I really like Trace because it
helps me see things like N+1

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:55.002
query errors.

00:15:55.002 --> 00:15:56.584
This is a really
common error that you

00:15:56.584 --> 00:15:59.110
see in things like Django and
Rails where you have a loop,

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:00.818
and inside the loop,
you're doing a query

00:16:00.818 --> 00:16:02.240
to get a single row.

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:04.560
So every time you go through
that loop, you do a query.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:07.060
And it's much more performant
to do the query ahead of time,

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:09.700
pull back all of the data,
and then iterate through it.

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:12.220
Because queries have
I/O implications,

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:13.570
and they take longer.

00:16:13.570 --> 00:16:16.557
And I have written apps
on Google Cloud Platform

00:16:16.557 --> 00:16:17.140
that had this.

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:19.780
And in Trace, it's really
obvious because it's Cloud

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:22.000
SQL, Cloud SQL, Cloud SQL, oh--

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:23.374
I should not do that.

00:16:23.374 --> 00:16:24.790
We also have
Stackdriver Profiler.

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:25.630
It's relatively new.

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:27.070
Everyone who is
using it loves it.

00:16:27.070 --> 00:16:29.440
It's another way to see
potential performance problems

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:31.560
in your application.

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:34.600
Metrics-- so when I
worked at this startup,

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:38.800
we actually had a cash register
sound that played in the office

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:40.180
every time we made a sale.

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:40.900
We were new.

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:41.759
It was exciting.

00:16:41.759 --> 00:16:43.300
And every time we
had to do a refund,

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:46.540
we played the sound backwards.

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:47.349
That was a metric.

00:16:47.349 --> 00:16:48.640
There are all kinds of metrics.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:51.452
Maybe it's simultaneous users,
or number of successful logins,

00:16:51.452 --> 00:16:53.910
or a number of times someone
has gone through your checkout

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:54.310
path.

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:56.559
There are all sorts of
metrics, depending on your app,

00:16:56.559 --> 00:16:58.840
there are ones that are
interesting or not to you.

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:00.460
The nice thing
about Stackdriver is

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:02.769
that you can take those
metrics out of your logs.

00:17:02.769 --> 00:17:04.810
Because there's a lot of
stuff you can't actually

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:07.660
pull out of system data,
but it's all over the logs.

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:10.900
And you can make those log
messages show up in aggregated

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:13.359
form-- averages, means, counts--

00:17:13.359 --> 00:17:14.932
in your dashboards as metrics.

00:17:14.932 --> 00:17:16.390
And then you can
do alerts on them.

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:17.264
So that's super cool.

00:17:17.264 --> 00:17:20.157
It's one of my favorite things
about Stackdriver right now.

00:17:20.157 --> 00:17:21.490
And then there's SLA monitoring.

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:23.230
We talked about it a bit
in one of the keynotes.

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:24.771
There have been
other talks about it.

00:17:24.771 --> 00:17:27.099
We have a blog post we
published last week on this.

00:17:27.099 --> 00:17:28.960
To do SLA monitoring
correctly, you

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:31.270
need to know what your SLA is.

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:33.790
So if you have a team, you
should sit down with your team,

00:17:33.790 --> 00:17:35.560
you should talk about
your happy path,

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:38.143
and you should talk about, what
is a reasonable amount of time

00:17:38.143 --> 00:17:39.820
that those requests
should be taking?

00:17:39.820 --> 00:17:43.004
This is a very
uncomfortable conversation.

00:17:43.004 --> 00:17:44.920
I recommend starting
with the data you already

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:46.499
have and decide, is this OK?

00:17:46.499 --> 00:17:48.040
If it's OK, you
should just make sure

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:49.810
that you know if it gets worse.

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:52.630
If it's not OK, well, you need
to put stuff in your work queue

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:53.597
to fix it.

00:17:53.597 --> 00:17:55.180
And then you should
make sure that you

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:57.020
know if you're getting worse.

00:17:57.020 --> 00:17:58.510
One of the places I worked,
we just had a stoplight.

00:17:58.510 --> 00:17:59.843
We had five requests that were--

00:17:59.843 --> 00:18:02.476
I think they made up
98% of our traffic.

00:18:02.476 --> 00:18:03.850
And we had a
stoplight because we

00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:06.340
knew we wanted the 95%
case on all of those

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:08.630
to be within a certain range.

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:10.410
And if it went
outside, the stoplight

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:13.460
turned red and that was bad.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:17.360
Question five-- so what
are the rough spots

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:18.310
of your application?

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:20.060
So some of you are
thinking, I don't write

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:21.143
code that has rough spots.

00:18:21.143 --> 00:18:23.480
And I'm thinking,
Awesome-- can I be you?

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:26.499
Because I write code that
has rough spots all the time.

00:18:26.499 --> 00:18:28.040
There are going to
be spots where you

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:29.390
didn't get as much QA coverage.

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:30.770
Maybe it was really
hard to test it.

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:32.395
Maybe it was a third
party integration.

00:18:32.395 --> 00:18:34.467
Maybe you hate automated
testing frameworks,

00:18:34.467 --> 00:18:36.300
and you know that you
just didn't get to it.

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:39.290
Maybe there's that one person on
your team who's, like, my code

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:39.830
is perfect.

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:41.240
I shouldn't have to test.

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:44.420
I'm not going to
follow the rules.

00:18:44.420 --> 00:18:45.200
Great.

00:18:45.200 --> 00:18:46.460
We all have rough spots.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:47.630
Google has rough spots.

00:18:47.630 --> 00:18:48.800
Everyone has rough spots.

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:50.312
Just know what they
are because you

00:18:50.312 --> 00:18:51.770
want to treat them
differently when

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:54.550
you're going to production.

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:55.964
You want to instrument them.

00:18:55.964 --> 00:18:58.255
So one of the things I am
very passionate about-- there

00:18:58.255 --> 00:18:59.420
are a couple of
things in this talk.

00:18:59.420 --> 00:19:00.794
But this is one
of my favorites--

00:19:00.794 --> 00:19:03.760
is you should know what the
rates of 5xx and 4xx errors are

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:06.207
compared to your
200's and your 300's.

00:19:06.207 --> 00:19:07.540
Here's why you should know that.

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:13.180
I have found a lot of
production plugs using this.

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:15.550
My two favorites are, I
was working for a company,

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:17.560
and marketing sent
out a marketing email

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:19.360
with a deep link to the website.

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:22.030
Problem is, we hadn't
implemented that deep link yet.

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:24.190
So all of the sudden,
our 404 rates spiked.

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:25.420
We had instrumentation on it.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:26.253
We had alerts on it.

00:19:26.253 --> 00:19:28.330
We're like, something
is not quite right here.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:29.770
Let's go fix that.

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:32.240
And we were able to really
quickly shove a redirect in

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:34.448
to make it so that all those
people that had received

00:19:34.448 --> 00:19:37.660
that marketing message,
after the first 50 or so,

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:38.680
got to the right place.

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:40.810
They didn't just see
a 404 and think, Well,

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:43.750
those guys are dumb.

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:45.640
Another story-- I was
working in consulting.

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:47.422
And we were doing a
project for a company,

00:19:47.422 --> 00:19:49.630
and we had just brought it
online for the first time.

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:51.790
And someone at our
contact at the company

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:54.290
hadn't told the IT department
that we were doing this.

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:57.005
So the IT guys or
gals sees a thing

00:19:57.005 --> 00:19:58.630
on the network they
hadn't done before.

00:19:58.630 --> 00:19:59.860
So they do the logical
thing, and they

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:02.110
start probing it trying to
figure out if it's secure,

00:20:02.110 --> 00:20:03.810
right-- totally makes sense.

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:05.470
And we're sitting
there going, What

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:08.260
are people doing
to our application?

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:10.000
Because the 404's, the 500's--

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.570
all of the errors start
spiking through the roof.

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:14.110
And so we do the
exact right thing

00:20:14.110 --> 00:20:16.568
you should do when someone's
trying to find vulnerabilities

00:20:16.568 --> 00:20:20.122
in your website, which is,
we blocked that IP address.

00:20:20.122 --> 00:20:22.330
And then we get a call
saying, Why did you guys block

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:24.190
the IT department's IP address?

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:27.022
Because they were doing mean
things to our application.

00:20:27.022 --> 00:20:28.480
But then we were
able to figure out

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:30.790
a time when they could do the
security scan, when we knew

00:20:30.790 --> 00:20:31.840
that they were doing
the security scan,

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:33.589
and it wasn't going
to affect development.

00:20:33.589 --> 00:20:34.901
So you know-- communication.

00:20:34.901 --> 00:20:37.150
But if that had been someone
from the big, wide, scary

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:38.637
internet, blocking
their IP address

00:20:38.637 --> 00:20:40.220
would have been the
right thing to do.

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:42.640
And we only knew about it
because we had this kind

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:45.370
of monitoring in place.

00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:47.669
Trace and Debugger-- two
features in Stackdriver--

00:20:47.669 --> 00:20:49.210
I've talked a little
bit about Trace.

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:50.920
Again, it will help
you find your errors.

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:53.320
Debugger is the
coolest thing we have.

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:54.825
I am so stoked about it.

00:20:54.825 --> 00:20:56.950
If you haven't played with
it, Stackdriver Debugger

00:20:56.950 --> 00:21:00.790
is live application
debugging in production.

00:21:00.790 --> 00:21:01.681
You install an agent.

00:21:01.681 --> 00:21:04.180
It sits in the background-- it
doesn't have a lot of latency

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:07.300
or any latency depending on the
language-- and does nothing.

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:10.300
And then you're like, how
the heck did that person get

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:11.710
into that section of the "If?"

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:12.350
I don't know.

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:13.664
That's not supposed to happen.

00:21:13.664 --> 00:21:15.580
And you're like, great,
Stackdriver Debugger--

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:16.722
you go in to the UI.

00:21:16.722 --> 00:21:18.430
You can either run it
completely locally,

00:21:18.430 --> 00:21:21.370
or you can hook your
cloud repo up to it,

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:23.440
shows you your code,
you hit a little button.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:25.810
You're like, tell me what
the state of the application

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:28.840
is when someone gets here.

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:31.300
And a request goes through,
maybe from you, maybe

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:32.630
from a customer.

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:35.252
And what we do is we
capture the call stack,

00:21:35.252 --> 00:21:37.210
all of the local variables,
all the information

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:38.800
that you probably
need to debug it,

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:42.100
and we print it out for you, and
then we let the request finish.

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:43.990
We don't give you
step-through debugging,

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:46.281
because that would be a
horrible experience for whoever

00:21:46.281 --> 00:21:48.210
was on the other
end of that request.

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:50.941
But we do give you a good
sized watch window and a set

00:21:50.941 --> 00:21:51.940
of information about it.

00:21:51.940 --> 00:21:54.410
And a feature we added in
the last couple of months,

00:21:54.410 --> 00:21:57.700
maybe year, is we have a
very small logging language

00:21:57.700 --> 00:22:00.700
that you can add arbitrary log
lines to an application that

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:01.742
is running in production.

00:22:01.742 --> 00:22:03.491
And the reason this
is cool is because you

00:22:03.491 --> 00:22:04.750
don't have to redeploy.

00:22:04.750 --> 00:22:05.800
You can do it all live.

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:07.210
You can do it all in the UI.

00:22:07.210 --> 00:22:09.860
You don't actually
have to push new code.

00:22:09.860 --> 00:22:12.370
And for anyone who's had to
instrument a bunch of code,

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:14.620
and then push it, and then
had all of those long lines

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:18.426
or whatever affect all of
your requests, this is magic.

00:22:18.426 --> 00:22:20.050
And I'm going to take
a drink of water.

00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:21.010
And some of you
should just think

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:22.718
about what we had to
do to make that work

00:22:22.718 --> 00:22:26.055
for compiled languages like
Java and Go because it's gross.

00:22:31.010 --> 00:22:32.870
Today, I'm like, wait, wait.

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:34.130
We have this for Java?

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:35.270
How did we do that?

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:38.720
And then they told me, and
I'm, like, yeah, that's gross.

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:41.069
And a lot of the
libraries that do this

00:22:41.069 --> 00:22:42.110
are actually open source.

00:22:42.110 --> 00:22:43.370
So you can go look
and see how we do it.

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:45.080
Like, for Ruby, this
was not scary to me.

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:46.670
Of course, we rewrote
classes at runtime.

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:47.390
We do that stuff.

00:22:47.390 --> 00:22:48.254
It's fine.

00:22:48.254 --> 00:22:50.420
And the other thing you
should do on your rough bits

00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:52.628
is you should make sure that
you shorten your release

00:22:52.628 --> 00:22:53.760
cycle as much as possible.

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:55.890
I've been debugging a lot
of stuff the last few weeks.

00:22:55.890 --> 00:22:57.830
And if I have to wait
15 minutes to figure out

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:00.190
if my change worked,
it is a bad day for me.

00:23:00.190 --> 00:23:02.440
So we talked about Cloud
Build in one of the keynotes.

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:04.690
It is my new
favoritest thing ever.

00:23:04.690 --> 00:23:06.260
You're like, here's
a bunch of steps.

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:08.049
Go do that on really
big machines, Google,

00:23:08.049 --> 00:23:10.340
and then put the stuff that
you build or make somewhere

00:23:10.340 --> 00:23:11.510
where I can grab it later.

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:13.430
It's on your network, so
it's on the same network

00:23:13.430 --> 00:23:14.690
as my production
harbor, so I don't

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:15.773
have to deal with latency.

00:23:15.773 --> 00:23:16.986
It's fantastic.

00:23:16.986 --> 00:23:18.860
There's a limited set
of stuff that's already

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:20.110
preconfigured for Cloud Build.

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:21.214
But watch this space.

00:23:21.214 --> 00:23:23.630
There are going to be more and
more templates and examples

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:25.546
coming from both the
community and from Google

00:23:25.546 --> 00:23:28.012
over the next several
months, maybe years.

00:23:28.012 --> 00:23:30.470
I work on the languages efforts,
specifically, in advocacy.

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:32.220
And a bunch of us are
trying to figure out

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:35.320
what the right templates to give
our language communities are.

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:38.390
App Engine Standard has a
relatively short release cycle.

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:41.930
You're like, please make
this new code go here.

00:23:41.930 --> 00:23:42.480
That's great.

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:43.839
Use that.

00:23:43.839 --> 00:23:45.255
And Cloud Functions,
again, I said

00:23:45.255 --> 00:23:46.940
it's one of my new
favoritest things.

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:49.370
I have been debugging some
really gross Cloud Functions

00:23:49.370 --> 00:23:51.920
because I'm not a particularly
good JavaScript developer.

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:55.100
And the fact that I can edit
it in the UI, hit the, Yeah--

00:23:55.100 --> 00:23:59.060
Try That button, and it spins
for a minute, maybe two, maybe

00:23:59.060 --> 00:23:59.930
only 30 seconds.

00:23:59.930 --> 00:24:02.680
And then there's a tab next
to it that says "Test."

00:24:02.680 --> 00:24:04.910
And I click that, and
it tries the function,

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:07.730
and then the logs for that
request spit out underneath it.

00:24:07.730 --> 00:24:08.420
It's great.

00:24:08.420 --> 00:24:10.010
I can iterate doing several--

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:13.640
I can probably do 5 to
10 different attempts

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:15.620
in an hour, maybe even more.

00:24:15.620 --> 00:24:16.380
It's fantastic.

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:19.690
OK.

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:23.184
So question six-- how
fault tolerant am I?

00:24:23.184 --> 00:24:25.100
So this is the one that
makes everyone really,

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:27.490
really nervous and really,
really not OK with the fact

00:24:27.490 --> 00:24:29.034
that I'm saying this.

00:24:29.034 --> 00:24:30.700
I'm going to tell you
right now that you

00:24:30.700 --> 00:24:34.060
have to accept that sometimes
things are going to go wrong.

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:37.030
Maybe it's not your
fault. Maybe a shark bit

00:24:37.030 --> 00:24:38.890
through the undersea cable.

00:24:38.890 --> 00:24:40.560
There is a story
about that happening,

00:24:40.560 --> 00:24:42.980
and you can go find
it on the internet.

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:45.556
But things that are bad
are going to happen.

00:24:45.556 --> 00:24:47.680
So you have to acknowledge
that things that are bad

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:48.513
are going to happen.

00:24:48.513 --> 00:24:51.550
And you have to decide what
you're willing to accept.

00:24:51.550 --> 00:24:53.290
If you've been to
any of the SRE talks,

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:55.540
you've probably heard about
the idea of error budgets.

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:56.740
I like error budgets.

00:24:56.740 --> 00:24:59.610
Error budgets are fantastic.

00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:01.740
Now, maybe you work
on an application

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:04.470
that keeps airplanes flying
and keeps people alive.

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:06.790
Awesome, you're amazing.

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:09.240
Your risk tolerance is
going to be lower than mine.

00:25:09.240 --> 00:25:10.680
I make websites.

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:13.280
It's fine if they go
down for a little while.

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:15.570
Nobody's going to die.

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:17.910
So think about what
your fault tolerance is.

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:20.637
Maybe you can deal with an
arbitrary or reasonable amount

00:25:20.637 --> 00:25:22.220
of downtime outside
of business hours,

00:25:22.220 --> 00:25:24.720
but you really need to stay
up during business hours.

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.855
Or maybe 15 minutes a
month is all you can offer.

00:25:26.855 --> 00:25:28.230
But you need to
know that, so you

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:30.484
can make the right decisions
about how much capacity

00:25:30.484 --> 00:25:32.650
you need to provision, how
much you need to monitor,

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:34.722
and all that.

00:25:34.722 --> 00:25:36.430
Once you know how
fault tolerant you are,

00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:38.013
you can work backwards,
and figure out

00:25:38.013 --> 00:25:39.490
what you should be monitoring.

00:25:39.490 --> 00:25:41.260
You should monitor
as thoroughly as you

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:43.510
need to to make sure that
you have achieved your fault

00:25:43.510 --> 00:25:46.730
tolerance goals.

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:48.940
So if you're not
particularly worried,

00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:50.980
uptime is probably
enough for you.

00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:53.815
But if performance is important
to your fault tolerance,

00:25:53.815 --> 00:25:55.690
if you need to know that
everything's working

00:25:55.690 --> 00:25:59.410
across data centers, if you
need to know latency on things,

00:25:59.410 --> 00:26:00.220
monitor the stuff.

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:02.140
Monitor the stuff that
can't fail, or monitor

00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:06.040
the stuff where you need to
talk to someone if it does fail.

00:26:06.040 --> 00:26:08.080
If you don't take anything
else from this talk,

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:10.750
please take the next two
points, and write them

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:13.030
millions of times
until you get it.

00:26:13.030 --> 00:26:16.000
Don't wake yourself up if you
can't do anything about it.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:17.914
Alerts must be actionable.

00:26:17.914 --> 00:26:19.330
My wonderful story
about this was,

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:21.784
I carried the pager for a
company for a couple of years.

00:26:21.784 --> 00:26:23.950
And one night at, like,
2:00 AM, the pager goes off,

00:26:23.950 --> 00:26:25.840
and, you know,
heartbeat goes up.

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:28.330
And the alert was "The
production disk on one

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:31.960
of the five production
servers is at 86% full."

00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:33.470
OK.

00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:35.000
And I go look at
the alert history.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:38.030
And I'm like, oh, five
days ago, it was 85% full.

00:26:38.030 --> 00:26:40.460
I don't have to do anything
about that at 2:00 AM.

00:26:40.460 --> 00:26:42.260
There is nothing
interesting happening.

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:45.750
We could wait several more
weeks, and it would be fine.

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:47.930
So I should not have gotten
woken up for that alert.

00:26:47.930 --> 00:26:49.395
That is an example
of a bad alert.

00:26:49.395 --> 00:26:51.020
It was also an alert
that I configured,

00:26:51.020 --> 00:26:54.500
so it's my own fault.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:56.870
But make sure that, if
you're alerting on something,

00:26:56.870 --> 00:26:59.245
that there is either something
technological that you can

00:26:59.245 --> 00:27:00.914
do to fix it, or
there is someone

00:27:00.914 --> 00:27:03.080
that you need to either be
telling that something is

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:04.940
going wrong or apologizing to.

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:06.459
That is also an action.

00:27:06.459 --> 00:27:08.000
But if there's
nothing that you would

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:09.958
do in the middle of the
night if this went off,

00:27:09.958 --> 00:27:11.630
if it can wait until
the next morning,

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:14.540
alert in an appropriate format.

00:27:14.540 --> 00:27:17.750
Also, alert on
what needs to work.

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:20.840
If you're OK with your
forgotten password link

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:23.130
not working for a
couple of hours,

00:27:23.130 --> 00:27:25.740
that should not
set your pager off.

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:28.410
Focus on the stuff that's really
important to work, and please

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:29.100
ignore the rest.

00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:31.404
Because pager fatigue
is totally a thing,

00:27:31.404 --> 00:27:32.820
and it's horrible
when you get it,

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:34.860
and it makes everyone ignore
everything that's going on.

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:37.260
And then something bad happens,
and everyone freaks out.

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:39.600
It's just-- no, please don't.

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:41.640
Your life will be
better if you don't.

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:43.770
I also like, when I'm setting up
a site, to have two dashboards.

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:45.940
I have a dashboard for my
happy path, my critical path.

00:27:45.940 --> 00:27:47.315
And then I have
another dashboard

00:27:47.315 --> 00:27:48.997
that has the entire
system holistically.

00:27:48.997 --> 00:27:50.580
And the reason I
have this is, someone

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:53.038
comes running over to my desk,
and tells me that everything

00:27:53.038 --> 00:27:55.630
is on fire, and I'm like,
oh, that is very interesting.

00:27:55.630 --> 00:27:57.450
And then I look up
at my two dashboards.

00:27:57.450 --> 00:28:00.447
And if the happy path dashboard
is all red and scary, yes,

00:28:00.447 --> 00:28:02.280
everything is on fire,
and I should probably

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:04.150
work on that right now.

00:28:04.150 --> 00:28:06.710
But if the holistic
dashboard is red and scary,

00:28:06.710 --> 00:28:09.140
but the happy path
is not red and scary,

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:10.824
I probably have a
little bit of time

00:28:10.824 --> 00:28:12.240
before stuff goes
really sideways.

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:14.995
So I can take a deep breath,
and gather my thoughts,

00:28:14.995 --> 00:28:16.620
and get to a place
where I can actually

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:18.990
deal with this in an
appropriate fashion.

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:21.620
I find it really helps
for me to separate--

00:28:21.620 --> 00:28:24.900
is everything on fire, or is
only the really important stuff

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:25.830
on fire?

00:28:25.830 --> 00:28:28.710
Or is the thing that's
on fire interesting?

00:28:28.710 --> 00:28:30.240
Because I like
when stuff breaks.

00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:35.130
It's cool, but not so urgent.

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:37.020
So I'm going talk about
disaster recovery.

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:39.311
I'm not going to give you a
ton of information on this.

00:28:39.311 --> 00:28:41.370
Go read stuff.

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:43.980
I'm going to tell you that all
the SREs I know would tell you

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:46.063
you should be in multiple
regions or zones, pretty

00:28:46.063 --> 00:28:46.837
please.

00:28:46.837 --> 00:28:47.670
It's better for you.

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:48.810
It's better for everyone.

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:51.270
Please make sure that you can
deploy in multiple regions

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:51.769
or zones.

00:28:51.769 --> 00:28:53.700
You don't have to
have them both live.

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:55.880
If you don't deploy in
multiple regions or zones,

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:58.380
make sure that you can deploy
to a new region or zone

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:00.540
relatively quickly if
being up relatively quickly

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:01.709
is important.

00:29:01.709 --> 00:29:03.000
Google had an outage last week.

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:05.590
It was a global outage, but
lots of other cloud providers,

00:29:05.590 --> 00:29:07.797
including us, have had
regional outages before.

00:29:07.797 --> 00:29:09.630
A good way to protect
yourself against those

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:12.572
is to be in multiple
regions or zones.

00:29:12.572 --> 00:29:14.780
It's also-- if redundancy
is really important to you,

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:15.930
if robustness is really
important to you,

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:17.827
pick one of the options
that's self-healing.

00:29:17.827 --> 00:29:19.410
Most of our things
can be self-healing

00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:21.540
if you configure
them to be that way.

00:29:21.540 --> 00:29:26.021
I love Kubernetes because I can
just delete VMs underneath it.

00:29:26.021 --> 00:29:28.020
And it's like, oh, there's
a VM that is deleted.

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:29.740
Let me please create a new one.

00:29:29.740 --> 00:29:31.920
It's just chill
about it; it's great.

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:34.050
I also-- if you're going
to do multiple regions,

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:36.180
one of my biggest pet
peeves about other systems

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:38.263
that I've used is that you
can only see one region

00:29:38.263 --> 00:29:39.900
at a time on your screen.

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:42.420
I really like that GCP-- this
is, like, my only marketing

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:43.160
thing here.

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:44.118
I really like that GCP.

00:29:44.118 --> 00:29:45.690
It shows you everything.

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:47.460
You saw, in the
keynote on Tuesday,

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:49.160
when they showed the
Kubernetes on-prem,

00:29:49.160 --> 00:29:51.690
that you could see two different
clusters at the same time

00:29:51.690 --> 00:29:52.740
in different regions.

00:29:52.740 --> 00:29:54.240
I can't tell you
the number of times

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:56.700
I have lost a VM because
I created it in a region

00:29:56.700 --> 00:30:00.480
that I'm not currently
looking at in other providers.

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:02.340
And then disaster
recovery is a complete lie

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:03.790
unless you practice it.

00:30:03.790 --> 00:30:05.865
I deleted a production
database at midnight.

00:30:05.865 --> 00:30:07.740
And the only reason that
I did not completely

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:09.840
freak out and lose
my brain was that I

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:13.140
had practiced restoring a
backup on my local machine

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:13.980
multiple times.

00:30:13.980 --> 00:30:15.750
I'd done it on a
staging machine.

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:18.630
So I took a deep breath,
got myself a glass of tea,

00:30:18.630 --> 00:30:21.870
and started the restore, and
then emailed my boss and said,

00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:24.330
sorry, we're going to
be out for a while.

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:28.352
I should have it back
up by 1:00, maybe 2:00.

00:30:28.352 --> 00:30:30.810
So the last thing I have on my
list of questions-- and this

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:33.100
is the one that
everyone messes up.

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:35.130
Where are my secrets?

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:37.007
Everyone's got secrets.

00:30:37.007 --> 00:30:39.090
Secrets might be things
like service account keys.

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:41.520
It may be API keys for
third-party services.

00:30:41.520 --> 00:30:44.010
And the big one is user info.

00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:46.620
Hi, I am likely to be
a user of at least one

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:49.240
of your guys's
products at one point.

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:50.960
Please don't leak my data.

00:30:50.960 --> 00:30:53.630
I would very much
appreciate that.

00:30:53.630 --> 00:30:55.170
So where do secrets end up?

00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:58.570
Secrets end up all
sorts of awesome places.

00:30:58.570 --> 00:31:00.160
Secrets end up in
source control.

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:03.520
I have checked in a GCP
service account before.

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:05.070
Yes, once you have
done that, you

00:31:05.070 --> 00:31:08.200
need to provision a new key, and
delete that, and never use it

00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:08.790
again.

00:31:08.790 --> 00:31:11.110
You have just shared it
with the entire internet.

00:31:11.110 --> 00:31:11.800
It's a password.

00:31:11.800 --> 00:31:13.472
We don't share those.

00:31:13.472 --> 00:31:15.930
Secrets frequently end up on
development and build servers.

00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:17.200
And you're like,
well, that's OK.

00:31:17.200 --> 00:31:18.700
Except, I worked at a
company where we had

00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:19.900
one of our dev boxes stolen.

00:31:19.900 --> 00:31:22.790
Someone stole it on a
subway or something.

00:31:22.790 --> 00:31:24.959
And if we had had any
user info or any API

00:31:24.959 --> 00:31:26.500
keys on that machine,
they would have

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:28.120
been immediately compromised.

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:31.035
So try not to let
secrets get on dev boxes.

00:31:31.035 --> 00:31:33.740
It is a really bad idea.

00:31:33.740 --> 00:31:35.110
The one that happens a lot--

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:37.300
a lot, a lot, a lot--
and has happened recently

00:31:37.300 --> 00:31:39.280
to a relatively
large tech company

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:40.930
is that secrets end up in logs.

00:31:40.930 --> 00:31:43.850
Frequently, this is
personal information.

00:31:43.850 --> 00:31:45.560
So think about it this way.

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:47.590
Someone submits a form.

00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:49.990
Do you log the post body?

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:53.380
A lot of frameworks and
applications do by default.

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:55.090
Anything in that
post body is now

00:31:55.090 --> 00:31:57.640
likely in plain
text in your logs.

00:31:57.640 --> 00:32:01.630
So my password, my credit card,
my address, all of that may

00:32:01.630 --> 00:32:03.590
have just been
dumped to your logs.

00:32:03.590 --> 00:32:06.610
No, we don't do that.

00:32:06.610 --> 00:32:07.540
I have done it.

00:32:07.540 --> 00:32:10.480
I felt very guilty about it,
and we fixed it within an hour.

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:13.550
It was a long time ago,
like, 15 years ago.

00:32:13.550 --> 00:32:14.860
Same thing with alerts--

00:32:14.860 --> 00:32:18.452
if you have a 500 error, do you
take a whole entire snapshot

00:32:18.452 --> 00:32:20.410
of the request and then
throw that in the alert

00:32:20.410 --> 00:32:22.340
that you're sending out
to the person on-call?

00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:24.797
Again, that's probably
got some stuff in it

00:32:24.797 --> 00:32:26.380
that you shouldn't
be doing that with.

00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:28.150
Because if you're sending it
out to an alerting system,

00:32:28.150 --> 00:32:30.608
you're automatically sharing
all of that info with whatever

00:32:30.608 --> 00:32:32.050
the alerting system is.

00:32:32.050 --> 00:32:32.720
Think about it.

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:34.178
I'm not saying you
shouldn't do it.

00:32:34.178 --> 00:32:35.980
But make sure you're
doing it consciously.

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:37.120
Email-- I worked
at a company where

00:32:37.120 --> 00:32:38.770
we emailed every
piece of user feedback

00:32:38.770 --> 00:32:40.480
to every employee
at the company.

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:41.020
No.

00:32:41.020 --> 00:32:42.607
That's a bad idea as well.

00:32:42.607 --> 00:32:44.440
We ended up running it
through an anonymizer

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:47.890
and then doing it, which
was actually quite awesome

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:49.717
because some of the
feedback was great.

00:32:49.717 --> 00:32:51.800
And then databases-- you're
like, well, of course,

00:32:51.800 --> 00:32:53.950
personal information
ends up in databases.

00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:56.860
Please see my comment about
dev boxes getting stolen.

00:32:56.860 --> 00:32:58.780
If you let people
install a production copy

00:32:58.780 --> 00:33:01.196
on their local machine, make
sure that everything in there

00:33:01.196 --> 00:33:02.020
is anonymized.

00:33:02.020 --> 00:33:04.630
Ideally, you would have a
separate database of test info

00:33:04.630 --> 00:33:07.070
that they could use for testing.

00:33:07.070 --> 00:33:08.725
So how do you
protect your secrets?

00:33:08.725 --> 00:33:11.350
There's some stuff that you just
have to do in your engineering

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:11.695
practices.

00:33:11.695 --> 00:33:13.611
They have to be done in
the application layer.

00:33:13.611 --> 00:33:15.760
I'm sorry, we don't have
magic stuff for that yet.

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:17.890
I am sure they will come
up with something soon.

00:33:17.890 --> 00:33:20.770
They seem to come up with
magic stuff all the time.

00:33:20.770 --> 00:33:24.130
But we do have the Metadata
Service and Kubernetes Secrets

00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:25.420
APIs.

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:27.940
Metadata Service is one of
my favorite things about GCP.

00:33:27.940 --> 00:33:31.400
I can say, here, put this
SSH key on all of my boxes.

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:32.785
Oh, that guy left.

00:33:32.785 --> 00:33:35.050
Take that SSH key off
of all of my boxes.

00:33:35.050 --> 00:33:36.759
And I don't have to
reprovision machines.

00:33:36.759 --> 00:33:38.133
Especially when
I was consulting,

00:33:38.133 --> 00:33:39.940
we were constantly
reprovisioning machines

00:33:39.940 --> 00:33:42.760
as people cycled on
and off the project.

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:44.040
You can also use a CLI.

00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:46.194
I like using the web
interface because I'm lazy.

00:33:46.194 --> 00:33:47.860
Kubernetes Secrets
is a little confusing

00:33:47.860 --> 00:33:49.992
because a lot of people
have opinions about it.

00:33:49.992 --> 00:33:51.700
But I've talked to
enough of those people

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:54.500
now that I actually believe the
way they're doing it is good.

00:33:54.500 --> 00:33:55.875
There are a couple
ways to do it.

00:33:55.875 --> 00:33:58.000
The way I like doing it is
you set up some secrets,

00:33:58.000 --> 00:33:59.590
and then you can access those.

00:33:59.590 --> 00:34:00.820
There are different
ways that you get access

00:34:00.820 --> 00:34:01.480
to that information.

00:34:01.480 --> 00:34:02.855
You can do
environment variables,

00:34:02.855 --> 00:34:04.950
or you can also have them
mounted as a file system

00:34:04.950 --> 00:34:06.660
that you can, then, pull from
as part of your container.

00:34:06.660 --> 00:34:08.035
There are a couple
ways to do it.

00:34:08.035 --> 00:34:08.739
It's great.

00:34:08.739 --> 00:34:09.880
Just take a deep
breath if you're like,

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:11.360
this is the weirdest
way to do this.

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:12.330
They've thought about it a lot.

00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:15.040
And this is one of the few times
I will say, give them a shot.

00:34:15.040 --> 00:34:17.770
You don't have to agree,
but at least try it.

00:34:17.770 --> 00:34:20.530
So I told you we were going
to have a review at the end.

00:34:20.530 --> 00:34:23.409
Compute-- Cloud Functions--

00:34:23.409 --> 00:34:24.429
you can use JavaScript.

00:34:24.429 --> 00:34:25.540
JavaScript is awesome.

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:28.570
Python is coming soon.

00:34:28.570 --> 00:34:30.909
It scales from zero to
lots and lots and lots.

00:34:30.909 --> 00:34:32.710
I believe that is
a technical term.

00:34:32.710 --> 00:34:35.920
I really like the speedy
deployment cycle on this one.

00:34:35.920 --> 00:34:38.500
And if you can get into
the small functions, lots

00:34:38.500 --> 00:34:41.230
of people working on small
pieces thing, it's great.

00:34:41.230 --> 00:34:43.063
Some people can't get
their heads around it.

00:34:43.063 --> 00:34:46.420
It took me a while, but
I think I'm there now.

00:34:46.420 --> 00:34:48.570
Compute Engine-- it's a VM.

00:34:48.570 --> 00:34:50.889
It can run stuff you
run on a computer.

00:34:50.889 --> 00:34:53.030
You can make it whatever
size and shape you want.

00:34:53.030 --> 00:34:55.126
We have sliders for that.

00:34:55.126 --> 00:34:56.500
If you're going
to do this, check

00:34:56.500 --> 00:34:57.820
out managed instance groups.

00:34:57.820 --> 00:34:59.740
They have health checks
that you can use.

00:34:59.740 --> 00:35:02.031
If you use the health checks,
you can get self-healing,

00:35:02.031 --> 00:35:03.440
and you can also
get autoscaling.

00:35:03.440 --> 00:35:05.395
And so you get all the
niceties that people

00:35:05.395 --> 00:35:07.270
talk about with some of
the managed services,

00:35:07.270 --> 00:35:09.116
but you just have it on a VM.

00:35:09.116 --> 00:35:11.110
VMs are familiar to many of us.

00:35:11.110 --> 00:35:12.370
We know how to make a VM work.

00:35:12.370 --> 00:35:13.072
It's awesome.

00:35:13.072 --> 00:35:15.280
And it's cheaper, but it
might need more of your time

00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:18.310
to handle it or
set up provisioning

00:35:18.310 --> 00:35:20.536
scripts in something like
Puppet, Chef, or Ansible.

00:35:20.536 --> 00:35:22.450
App Engine-- we
have two flavors--

00:35:22.450 --> 00:35:23.776
standard and flexible.

00:35:23.776 --> 00:35:24.900
It's really easy to deploy.

00:35:24.900 --> 00:35:26.200
You type "gcloud app deploy."

00:35:26.200 --> 00:35:27.790
If it's one of the languages
we know, we say, It

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:29.710
looks like you're
running blah, would you

00:35:29.710 --> 00:35:32.620
like us to write a
configuration file for that?

00:35:32.620 --> 00:35:35.230
It supports all
languages in some form,

00:35:35.230 --> 00:35:38.360
and it supports anything you
can run in a container on Flex.

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:40.090
App Engine Standard
scales down to zero,

00:35:40.090 --> 00:35:41.965
so anything that runs
on App Engine Standard,

00:35:41.965 --> 00:35:44.320
you can scale down to zero,
but it has restrictions

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:46.834
on what libraries you can use.

00:35:46.834 --> 00:35:48.750
Logging, error reporting,
and basic monitoring

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:50.510
come by default with
App Engine Standard,

00:35:50.510 --> 00:35:52.760
and they're very easy to
set up for App Engine Flex.

00:35:52.760 --> 00:35:55.010
And it does the load balancing
and networking for you.

00:35:55.010 --> 00:35:56.676
My least favorite
part of running on VMs

00:35:56.676 --> 00:35:57.770
is setting up networking.

00:35:57.770 --> 00:36:00.310
And so, if someone else
does that, that's great.

00:36:00.310 --> 00:36:02.974
Kubernetes Engine--
it runs containers.

00:36:02.974 --> 00:36:05.140
I was supposed to have a
picture of a container ship

00:36:05.140 --> 00:36:07.750
or something on
this slide, but eh.

00:36:07.750 --> 00:36:10.330
It does autoscaling
and self-healing.

00:36:10.330 --> 00:36:12.940
It can't scale down to zero,
but it can scale down to not

00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:13.965
very much.

00:36:13.965 --> 00:36:15.340
There are no
restrictions on what

00:36:15.340 --> 00:36:17.740
you can run as long as you
can put it in a container.

00:36:17.740 --> 00:36:19.720
And it does the load balancing
and networking for you.

00:36:19.720 --> 00:36:21.030
So I actually end up
running a lot of stuff

00:36:21.030 --> 00:36:22.720
in Kubernetes
Engine because I get

00:36:22.720 --> 00:36:25.720
to run whatever I want
in whatever way I want,

00:36:25.720 --> 00:36:27.370
and it does service
discovery for me,

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:29.745
and I don't have to set up
load balancing and networking.

00:36:29.745 --> 00:36:31.130
It's fantastic.

00:36:31.130 --> 00:36:33.650
Stackdriver-- the other main
thing I talked about today--

00:36:33.650 --> 00:36:37.914
this is our operations,
monitoring, logging, debugging,

00:36:37.914 --> 00:36:39.580
it "helps you make
stuff better and know

00:36:39.580 --> 00:36:41.920
what's going on" platform.

00:36:41.920 --> 00:36:43.720
Stackdriver
Monitoring-- alerting

00:36:43.720 --> 00:36:45.730
via many different means,
you can get emails.

00:36:45.730 --> 00:36:48.520
You can get something
dumped into pager duty

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:51.280
or into one of your
team chat apps.

00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:53.340
You can set up
custom dashboards.

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:55.090
You have system-level
monitoring-- so

00:36:55.090 --> 00:36:56.119
how much CPU am I using?

00:36:56.119 --> 00:36:58.660
How many bytes are going over
the wire-- but also application

00:36:58.660 --> 00:37:01.150
level monitoring-- what's
my ratio of errors?

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:02.860
What's my average latency?

00:37:02.860 --> 00:37:05.620
What's the latency
of the 95% case?

00:37:05.620 --> 00:37:08.590
Because we all know that, if
you just look at the mean,

00:37:08.590 --> 00:37:11.500
that one request that
took five minutes

00:37:11.500 --> 00:37:13.720
is just going to
break everything.

00:37:13.720 --> 00:37:16.834
It's multi-cloud-- you
can use it on AWS and GCP.

00:37:16.834 --> 00:37:18.250
And for a lot of
the features, you

00:37:18.250 --> 00:37:19.958
can actually use that
on-prem or anywhere

00:37:19.958 --> 00:37:22.630
you can run your stuff if
you install the libraries.

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:25.060
If you're going to do
this, remember alerts

00:37:25.060 --> 00:37:26.182
should be actionable.

00:37:26.182 --> 00:37:28.390
And you should probably set
up something that vaguely

00:37:28.390 --> 00:37:30.389
looks like a system
overview, so you can quickly

00:37:30.389 --> 00:37:32.530
know what the heck is going on.

00:37:32.530 --> 00:37:34.510
Stackdriver Logging--
log aggregation--

00:37:34.510 --> 00:37:37.150
you can search stuff,
pretty good query language.

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:38.770
You can export to
files or you can

00:37:38.770 --> 00:37:42.537
export to BigQuery, which is
really, super, crazy powerful.

00:37:42.537 --> 00:37:44.620
You can feed the logs into
metrics and monitoring.

00:37:44.620 --> 00:37:46.210
I talked about that earlier.

00:37:46.210 --> 00:37:47.950
Again, multi-cloud--
use our libraries.

00:37:47.950 --> 00:37:50.830
You can also use it on-prem
if you use our libraries.

00:37:50.830 --> 00:37:53.560
Tips-- handle your
secrets with care.

00:37:53.560 --> 00:37:55.180
Please don't dump
secrets to logs

00:37:55.180 --> 00:37:59.177
unless you can control access
to those very, very well.

00:37:59.177 --> 00:38:01.510
Trace-- this is the tracing
through microservices thing.

00:38:01.510 --> 00:38:03.520
This lets you see the
timeline of what's

00:38:03.520 --> 00:38:05.047
going on in your application.

00:38:05.047 --> 00:38:07.630
It also has cool features like
period-over-period performance.

00:38:07.630 --> 00:38:10.000
If someone's like, the
website is slower this week,

00:38:10.000 --> 00:38:11.850
and you can say, No.

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:13.972
I can impose a
latency graph over top

00:38:13.972 --> 00:38:15.180
from last week and this week.

00:38:15.180 --> 00:38:16.510
And the answer to that is no.

00:38:16.510 --> 00:38:18.310
Or you can say, oh
yeah, sorry about that.

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:19.710
I'll fix it.

00:38:19.710 --> 00:38:22.180
You can also debug bottlenecks
and timeouts quickly.

00:38:22.180 --> 00:38:24.040
For some sets of
errors, we may actually

00:38:24.040 --> 00:38:26.000
predict what the
problem is and say,

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:29.561
hey, you might consider
adding an index here.

00:38:29.561 --> 00:38:31.060
But you need to
instrument your app,

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:32.935
unless you're running
on App Engine Standard.

00:38:32.935 --> 00:38:35.020
And the recommended agent's
going to be different

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:35.980
depending on your language.

00:38:35.980 --> 00:38:37.771
For some, we're using
OpenCensus, for some,

00:38:37.771 --> 00:38:39.664
we're using Stackdriver
Trace Agents.

00:38:39.664 --> 00:38:41.830
I believe were moving in
the direction of OpenCensus

00:38:41.830 --> 00:38:43.699
for everything.

00:38:43.699 --> 00:38:46.240
Stackdriver Debugger-- this is
the live production debugging.

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:47.770
Please go and try this.

00:38:47.770 --> 00:38:50.640
No one believes me,
and it is amazing.

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:52.390
But again, you need
to instrument your app

00:38:52.390 --> 00:38:52.970
with the agent.

00:38:52.970 --> 00:38:54.290
A lot of people get a
little twitchy about that.

00:38:54.290 --> 00:38:55.150
They're like, well,
why should I have

00:38:55.150 --> 00:38:56.170
this running in the background?

00:38:56.170 --> 00:38:57.730
You should have it running
in the background so

00:38:57.730 --> 00:38:59.229
that, when something
goes wrong, you

00:38:59.229 --> 00:39:02.400
don't have to do another
relay cycle to debug it.

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:06.390
And then Stackdriver Error
Reporting is crash aggregation.

00:39:06.390 --> 00:39:08.130
Something broke.

00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:10.140
It groups all the stuff
that's vaguely similar,

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:12.900
it takes representative traces,
it can link you to the logs,

00:39:12.900 --> 00:39:14.790
it can link to your bug tracker.

00:39:14.790 --> 00:39:17.219
So you can start from,
something is wrong,

00:39:17.219 --> 00:39:19.260
and then look at all the
other tools that are all

00:39:19.260 --> 00:39:20.640
linked together to debug it.

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:23.640
If you've ever had to assemble
a performance monitoring,

00:39:23.640 --> 00:39:25.980
regular monitoring,
log aggregation tool

00:39:25.980 --> 00:39:27.450
from a bunch of
different toolkits,

00:39:27.450 --> 00:39:29.940
having everything talk to each
other is really, really nice

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:32.600
and saves a lot of time.

00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:34.120
It has alert backoff built in.

00:39:34.120 --> 00:39:36.300
So if you do something
really stupid,

00:39:36.300 --> 00:39:37.980
and you're getting
a lot of alerts,

00:39:37.980 --> 00:39:40.220
you won't actually have
your pocket explode on you

00:39:40.220 --> 00:39:43.021
every time someone hits
the path that's broken.

00:39:43.021 --> 00:39:45.270
And it's automatically set
up for App Engine Standard.

00:39:45.270 --> 00:39:47.228
It's really easy to set
up for App Engine Flex.

00:39:47.228 --> 00:39:50.960
You just get grab the library,
and you shove it in your app.

00:39:50.960 --> 00:39:53.055
Stackdriver Profiler--
people are super into this.

00:39:53.055 --> 00:39:54.610
It helps you improve
code performance.

00:39:54.610 --> 00:39:56.235
So that's probably
why they're into it.

00:39:56.235 --> 00:39:58.680
It's also really easy to
use and uses flame graphs

00:39:58.680 --> 00:40:00.240
that people are used to.

00:40:00.240 --> 00:40:02.340
We have it for Java,
Go, and Node right now.

00:40:02.340 --> 00:40:04.090
I would assume more is coming.

00:40:04.090 --> 00:40:07.020
I can't actually give
you a timeline on that.

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:08.580
And that's what I have.

00:40:08.580 --> 00:40:09.610
Thank you for coming.

00:40:09.610 --> 00:40:10.710
I know it's the end
of the second day,

00:40:10.710 --> 00:40:12.251
and this is a giant,
crazy conference

00:40:12.251 --> 00:40:13.412
and very overwhelming.

00:40:13.412 --> 00:40:14.370
I appreciate your time.

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:15.810
Again, I'm Aja.

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:19.630
I'm on the Application
Development Advocacy Team.

00:40:19.630 --> 00:40:21.397
So I am not an Ops
person, but I'm

00:40:21.397 --> 00:40:22.980
happy to answer any
of your questions.

00:40:22.980 --> 00:40:26.130
You can email me at Thagomizer--
just spelled exactly how it

00:40:26.130 --> 00:40:27.960
sounds-- @google.com.

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:31.052
Or you can tweet at
me, @the_thagomizer.

00:40:31.052 --> 00:40:32.760
And I have a couple
of minutes at the end

00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:34.980
after I'm done talking, to hand
out some stickers to people,

00:40:34.980 --> 00:40:37.122
but I actually have an
appointment very shortly.

00:40:37.122 --> 00:40:38.080
So thank you very much.

00:40:38.080 --> 00:40:39.450
I appreciate your attention.

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:40.950
